377 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 214 0 R
/T (The amazing life of beetles: A four-stage journey)
/E (The amazing life of beetles: A four-stage journey)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Have you ever seen a ladybug, a June bug, or even a mealworm?)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (These are all types of beetles, and they have an amazing secret: they go through a complete transformation in their lives! This special kind of development is called "complete metamorphosis," and it's why beetles, including our mealworm friends, have four major life stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Egg Stage: This is where it all begins! Female beetles lay tiny eggs, often in a safe spot where the future babies will have food when they hatch. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Larva Stage: Once the egg hatches, out comes the larvae! For mealworms, this is the "mealworm" stage you're familiar with. Larvae are constantly eating to grow big and store up energy. They shed their skin multiple times as they get larger, which is called molting.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 214 0 R
/E (Pupa Stage: After the larva has eaten enough and grown to its full size, it enters the pupa stage. This is a resting stage where incredible changes happen inside, like a secret transformation. The pupa usually doesn't move or eat. Its purpose is to transform the insect from a worm-like larva into a winged adult.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
